WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.240
An active kinase sits in your cell membrane all day, yet the cell stays quiet.

2
00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:13.800
What holds it back until TGF-β -beta arrives, and how does the message reach DNA so

3
00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:14.800
fast?

4
00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:20.970
Many growth factor relays chain several enzymes inside the cell before any gene

5
00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:25.470
responds. Transforming growth factor β takes a shortcut.

6
00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:30.710
Its receptors are serine/threonine kinases of two types.

7
00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:36.270
Type II stays active all the time, like a lit match waiting in the membrane.

8
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.160
At rest, type I cannot fire on its own.

9
00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.400
A short inner stretch called the GS domain folds shut, capped by a small safety

10
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.440
catch called FKBP12.

11
00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:53.820
Now an amber TGF-β beta dimer arrives from outside the cell.

12
00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:01.060
It binds two cyan type II chains first, then pulls two indigo type I chains into a

13
00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:02.160
four-receptor complex.

14
00:01:03.333 --> 00:01:04.653
Pause and predict.

15
00:01:06.353 --> 00:01:11.333
Suppose a mutation breaks the type II kinase so it cannot transfer phosphate, while

16
00:01:11.333 --> 00:01:12.693
type I stays normal.

17
00:01:13.993 --> 00:01:19.113
When TGF-β -beta arrives, will the receptors still assemble, and will type I get

18
00:01:19.113 --> 00:01:20.113
phosphorylated?

19
00:01:25.883 --> 00:01:31.123
The four chains still assemble outside, because ligand binding happens on the outer

20
00:01:31.123 --> 00:01:36.663
domains. Inside, nothing happens, because type I cannot light itself.

21
00:01:38.003 --> 00:01:42.863
Restore the type II kinase, and bright yellow phosphates jump onto the GS domain.

22
00:01:44.523 --> 00:01:48.323
FKBP12 pops off, and the type I kinase opens active.

23
00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:54.387
That phosphorylated GS stretch now docks the next protein in line, a Smad.

24
00:01:55.647 --> 00:01:58.927
Remarkably, a Smad is itself a transcription factor.

25
00:02:00.327 --> 00:02:04.687
Active type I adds two phosphates to serines at the end of the Smad tail.

26
00:02:05.987 --> 00:02:09.567
The receptor just signalled straight to a DNA-binding protein.

27
00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:14.660
Each phosphorylated tail fits a pocket on a neighbouring Smad.

28
00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:21.260
Typically, two phosphorylated Smads leave the receptor and join one shared partner,

29
00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:27.680
violet Smad4. This three-part complex accumulates in the nucleus to meet DNA.

30
00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:34.300
Meanwhile, active type I keeps phosphorylating more Smads until brakes intervene.

31
00:02:35.533 --> 00:02:39.253
The family splits into two branches built on one design.

32
00:02:40.673 --> 00:02:46.213
TGF-β -beta and activin receptors phosphorylate emerald Smad2 and Smad3.

33
00:02:47.613 --> 00:02:53.993
Bone morphogenetic protein, or BMP, receptors phosphorylate coral Smad1, 5, and 8.

34
00:02:55.253 --> 00:03:01.353
Some BMPs grip type I tighter, yet phosphate still flows from type II, to type I, to

35
00:03:01.353 --> 00:03:02.353
the Smad.

36
00:03:02.867 --> 00:03:06.587
Notice the shared logic across both branches of the family.

37
00:03:08.047 --> 00:03:09.887
TGF-β -beta is not an enzyme.

38
00:03:11.247 --> 00:03:16.527
It bridges always-active type II and locked type I, sending phosphate one way to a

39
00:03:16.527 --> 00:03:22.607
Smad. Next, see how that same Smad message gives opposite answers in different

40
00:03:22.607 --> 00:03:22.927
cells.
